{0 Migrating from 0.2 to 0.3}

Awskit 0.3 is an intentionally breaking API cleanup. It keeps the runtime
agnostic package split, but makes configured clients, outbound domains,
ownership, result records, and secret handoffs explicit. Apply the
{{!page-migration_0_1_to_0_2}0.1 to 0.2 guide} first when upgrading from 0.1.

The left-hand snippets show 0.2 and the right-hand snippets show 0.3. The
examples use the public adapter APIs; runtime implementors should also read the
runtime-adapter signatures in [Awskit_s3.Runtime_adapter].

{1 Ready-made adapters keep one-step client construction}

Applications using a supplied Cohttp Lwt client should use
[Awskit_s3_lwt.Make]. Its [create] helper configured both the runtime and S3
in 0.2, and it continues to do so in 0.3. Do not compose [Awskit_lwt.Make]
and [Awskit_s3.Make] separately for this case.

{[(* 0.2 *)
module S3 = Awskit_s3_lwt.Make (My_cohttp_client)

let s3 =
  S3.create ~region:"us-east-1" ~credentials ~clock ?endpoint_config ()
  |> Result.get_ok

let result = S3.Object.get_string s3 ~bucket ~key ~max_bytes:1_048_576L ()
]}

{[(* 0.3 *)
module S3 = Awskit_s3_lwt.Make (My_cohttp_client)

let s3 =
  S3.create ~region:"us-east-1" ~credentials ~clock ?endpoint_config ()
  |> Result.get_ok

let result = S3.Object.get_string s3 ~bucket ~key ~max_bytes:1_048_576L ()
]}

The same one-step path is available through the ready-made Eio and Lwt Unix
adapters. Their returned [s3] value remains the configured operation client.

{1 Custom runtimes bind S3 policy after runtime construction}

This changes only direct users of [Awskit_s3.Make]. In 0.2 its input had to
implement [Awskit_s3.RUNTIME], which extended the generic runtime with an
[S3_endpoint] module, and operations received the resulting raw runtime
connection. In 0.3 the functor accepts the ordinary [Awskit.Runtime.S]
contract; [S3.create] binds the S3-specific endpoint policy to a runtime
connection once, and operations receive that configured S3 client.

{[(* 0.2 *)
module Runtime = struct
  include My_runtime

  module S3_endpoint = struct
    type nonrec connection = connection

    let s3_endpoint_config _ = endpoint_config
  end
end

module S3 = Awskit_s3.Make (Runtime)

let result =
  S3.Object.get_string runtime ~bucket ~key ~max_bytes:1_048_576L ()
]}

{[(* 0.3 *)
module S3 = Awskit_s3.Make (My_runtime)

let s3 = S3.create ~endpoint_config runtime

let result =
  S3.Object.get_string s3 ~bucket ~key ~max_bytes:1_048_576L ()
]}

This separates generic transport policy from S3 addressing and signing policy,
and allows one runtime connection to back independently configured S3 clients.
[Awskit_s3.Runtime_adapter] is the advanced implementor contract, not a
replacement for the configured operation surface.

{1 Root aliases and endpoint policy}

Use the core modules directly. The old [Awskit_s3.Region],
[Awskit_s3.Credentials], [Awskit_s3.Endpoint], and endpoint-resolution request
helpers are no longer part of the ordinary S3 facade.

{[(* 0.2 *)
let region = Awskit_s3.Region.of_string_exn "us-east-1"
let endpoint = Awskit_s3.Endpoint.https_exn ~host:"s3.example.test" ()
let credentials = Awskit_s3.Credentials.create_exn ~access_key_id ~secret_access_key ()
]}

{[(* 0.3 *)
let region = Awskit.Region.of_string_exn "us-east-1"
let endpoint = Awskit.Endpoint.https_exn ~host:"s3.example.test" ()
let credentials =
  Awskit.Credentials.create_exn ~access_key_id ~secret_access_key ()
]}

Endpoint policy builders are also stricter: [Endpoint_config.aws] returns a
result and rejects path-style transfer acceleration, while
[local_plaintext] no longer takes a redundant addressing-style argument.
Handle the result at construction time and use [s3_compatible] or
[unsafe_plaintext] when a non-loopback service really needs a different policy.

{[(* 0.2 *)
let endpoint_config =
  Awskit_s3.Endpoint_config.aws ~endpoint_variant:`Accelerate
    ~addressing_style:`Virtual_hosted ()
]}

{[(* 0.3 *)
let endpoint_config =
  Awskit_s3.Endpoint_config.aws ~endpoint_variant:`Accelerate
    ~addressing_style:`Virtual_hosted ()
  |> Result.get_ok
]}

{1 Option builders and direct operation arguments}

0.3 removes one-field option wrappers and fake fallibility. Operations with
only an expected owner now take [~expected_bucket_owner] directly, and option
builders return plain values when all supplied fields are already validated.
Keep using result-returning constructors for values that can genuinely fail,
such as metadata collections, checksum values, endpoint policy, and completion
part collections.

{[(* 0.2 *)
let options =
  Awskit_s3.Object.Get.options ~expected_bucket_owner:owner ()
  |> Result.get_ok

let head = S3.Object.head s3 ~bucket ~key ~options ()
]}

{[(* 0.3 *)
let options = Awskit_s3.Object.Get.options ~expected_bucket_owner:owner ()
let head = S3.Object.head s3 ~bucket ~key ~options ()

let bucket_head =
  S3.Bucket.head s3 ~bucket ~expected_bucket_owner:owner ()
]}

For [DeleteObjects], construct the validated non-empty collection before the
request. This replaces passing a raw list and makes the 1--1,000 member bound
explicit.

{[(* 0.2 *)
S3.Object.delete_objects s3 ~bucket ~objects:[ first; second ] ()
]}

{[(* 0.3 *)
let objects =
  Awskit_s3.Object.Delete_many.Objects.of_list_exn [ first; second ]

S3.Object.delete_objects s3 ~bucket ~objects ()
]}

{1 Result records}

Object GET results now group response metadata under [result.info], so the
consumed value and the metadata have stable, separate names.

{[(* 0.2 *)
match S3.Object.get_string s3 ~bucket ~key ~max_bytes:1_048_576L () with
| Ok result ->
    use result.value;
    log_etag result.etag
| Error error -> report error
]}

{[(* 0.3 *)
match S3.Object.get_string s3 ~bucket ~key ~max_bytes:1_048_576L () with
| Ok result ->
    use result.value;
    log_etag result.info.etag
| Error error -> report error
]}

Transfer results retain the strategy tag but expose byte counts and inert
identity fields. A completed multipart result no longer contains a reusable
upload handle; persist or resume a caller-owned handle before completion when
future operations need the upload identity.

{[(* 0.2 *)
match upload_result with
| Awskit_s3.Transfer.Multipart { upload; complete; _ } ->
    use upload;
    use complete
| Awskit_s3.Transfer.Put result -> use result
]}

{[(* 0.3 *)
match upload_result with
| Awskit_s3.Transfer.Multipart result ->
    use result.bucket;
    use result.key;
    use result.upload_id;
    use result.bytes_transferred;
    use result.complete
| Awskit_s3.Transfer.Put result ->
    use result.put;
    use result.bytes_transferred
]}

{1 Multipart ownership and completion invariants}

Only runtime adapters and simulators may construct an Awskit-created upload
handle. Application code resumes a persisted upload with an explicit
caller-owned handle. Completion lists are validated once and then passed as an
abstract collection.

{[(* 0.2 *)
let upload =
  Awskit_s3.Multipart.Upload.created ~bucket ~key ~upload_id

let part_number = Awskit_s3.Multipart.Part_number.of_int_exn 1
let part =
  Awskit_s3.Multipart.Part.create_exn ~part_number ~etag ()

S3.Multipart.complete_upload s3 ~upload ~parts:[ part ] ()
]}

{[(* 0.3 *)
let upload = Awskit_s3.Multipart.Upload.resume ~bucket ~key ~upload_id
let part_number = Awskit_s3.Multipart.Part_number.of_int_exn 1
let part =
  Awskit_s3.Multipart.Part.create_exn ~part_number ~etag ()
let parts = Awskit_s3.Multipart.Complete.Parts.of_list_exn [ part ]

S3.Multipart.complete_upload s3 ~upload ~parts ()
]}

Use [Multipart.Upload.Runtime_adapter.created] only inside an adapter or
simulator after [CreateMultipartUpload] succeeds. Use [Multipart.Upload.resume]
for a persisted [caller_owned] upload; a terminal result's [upload_id] is
identity data, not a live handle.

{1 Presigning and secrets}

Configured presigning now follows the same operation domains as ordinary S3
calls. Standalone signing uses [Presigned.Signer]. Signed header names are
separate from the deliberately revealed header values, and secret material
such as SSE-C headers is available only through an explicit [reveal_*] call.

{[(* 0.2 *)
let request =
  S3.Presigned.get_object s3 ~bucket ~key ()
  |> Result.get_ok

let headers = Awskit_s3.Presigned.request_headers request
let url = Awskit_s3.Presigned.reveal_url request
]}

{[(* 0.3 *)
let request =
  S3.Presigned.get_object s3 ~bucket ~key ()
  |> Result.get_ok

let header_names = Awskit_s3.Presigned.request_header_names request
let headers = Awskit_s3.Presigned.reveal_request_headers request
let url = Awskit_s3.Presigned.reveal_url request
]}

Use [safe_uri], [signed_header_names], [request_header_names], and [pp] for
logs and diagnostics. For customer-key encryption, replace direct key accessors
with [Encryption.Customer_key.reveal_headers] or
[reveal_copy_source_headers], and keep the returned values only for the HTTP
handoff.

{1 Managed transfer options}

Managed uploads now accept one object policy and derive matching single-request
and multipart options. This prevents crossing the threshold from dropping
metadata, tags, storage class, encryption, preconditions, or expected-owner
checks.

{[(* 0.2 *)
let options =
  Awskit_s3.Transfer.upload_options_exn
    ~part_size:16_777_216
    ~put_options
    ~create_options
    ~upload_part_options
    ~complete_options
    ~abort_options
    ~list_parts_options
    ()
]}

{[(* 0.3 *)
let options =
  Awskit_s3.Transfer.upload_options_exn
    ~part_size:16_777_216
    ~content_type
    ~metadata
    ~storage_class
    ~tags
    ~preconditions
    ~encryption
    ~expected_bucket_owner
    ()
]}

Managed file helpers no longer compute explicit checksum values. Use direct
[Object.put] or multipart operations when the application already has a
checksum value to send; use the transfer policy for transport strategy and
shared object semantics.
